    "summary": "Acoustic gems, Lovelight peaks, high‑energy electric set",
    "blurb": "The show opens with a beautifully tight acoustic set featuring first‑time performances of Ripple and Brokedown Palace. The second half erupts into a high‑energy electric stretch highlighted by China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider, Good Lovin' and a soaring Turn On Your Lovelight. Jerry’s pedal‑steel work and Pigpen’s piano add extra sparkle.",
    "review": "On August 19, 1970 at the Fillmore West the Dead delivered one of their most memorable early‑summer outings. The acoustic set is a standout – listeners rave about the historic first renditions of “Ripple” and “Brokedown Palace,” plus Pigpen’s surprisingly lively piano on “How Long Blues” and his honky‑tonk touch throughout. Jerry Garcia layers pedal‑steel wizardry into songs like “New Speedway Boogie” and provides tasteful lead work on the soaring second‑set “Good Lovin'.” The electric half never loses momentum, with a seamless flow from “China Cat Sunflower” into “I Know You Rider,” a blistering “Saint Stephen,” and the climactic double‑take of “Good Lovin’” that leads into a rousing “Turn On Your Lovelight.” Phil Lesh’s bass is especially clear on this audience tape, thanks to Taback’s mic placement, while the drums keep a solid groove despite the lack of detailed commentary. Overall, the performance captures the band at a creative peak, and the recommended Taback recording presents the clearest picture of that magic.",
